docs/architecture.md:29:`assets/sound-window.ts` decodes native MP3 at its tag rate before AssetPack becomes ready, crops the positive source seek/sample-count window and prepares local16-bit PCM WAV blobs. Browser decoding stays outside simulation. `AudioManager` consumes events through its ten general sound slots and advances music from the shared stage before World steps, retaining source transition acceptance and muted-track memory. Fresh menu/game construction issues a distinct music request; profile settings only change enablement. Retained Settings, creator and nested viewer visits do not invent scene requests. Music completion restarts the same media carrier at full source volume without resetting the stage transition counter; replaced, muted and disposed voices cannot restart through stale callbacks. `simulation/collision-sound.ts` dispatches the recovered virtual hit-sound policy and callback enemy argument; World advances its shared hit cooldown at the successful Run tail, including pause. An interrupted listener skips that tail. The browser does not apply a second hit throttle.
docs/architecture.md:33:The active frame checks progression in the interface phase, after existing-avatar controls/HUD and before field combat. Ordinary upgrades/downgrades therefore observe the previous field frame's final health. Level-zero damage starts its escape immediately. Primary input and passive weapons use separate category passes through `tickGuns`; passive shots are born after the field's bullet loop. Primary triggers run before field combat; primary cooldowns/aim and passive firing run in the later avatar phase. HUD readouts are captured in the interface phase, so later health/counter changes cannot leak into the same displayed frame. Shared secondary readiness retains its distinct zero reset frame and advances during transit. Dialog return modes retain an in-progress crossing through upgrade choice and secondary help. Boundary detection precedes interface work; the initiating state0 frame still runs the avatar, while subsequent state2 frames run both fields without player status/passive updates. Arrival clears movement and the player shield, discards all registered effects/callbacks, and resets primary counters. The fidelity ledger identifies remaining source quirks, weapon callbacks and independent animation-clock work.
docs/architecture.md:39:`simulation/wall-timers.ts` models elapsed-millisecond Flash Timer callbacks separately from fixed-step part counters. The production viewport advances it from actual RAF elapsed time and passes zero elapsed milliseconds into each fixed stage step; deterministic callers can use `World.step(input, milliseconds)` (default20ms). The player launcher starts ten100ms callbacks; ordinary crossings and avatar replacement cancel them, while pause/jam/stun and death escape do not. Transit rejects insertion without replaying consumed callbacks. Managed Massive Cannon/shield timelines advance once per stage step even while paused, loop at their authored length, and are polled only by active world modes. Completion follows field bullets. This models source lifecycle; exact Flash Timer/event-loop delivery under stalls remains a runtime comparison boundary.
docs/architecture.md:170:`simulation/forcefield.ts` separates wrapper attachment/generation and its stage-clock origin from damage capacity and recharge counters. Ordinary crossing cleanup detaches it; an eligible passive Run reconstructs it. Death escape and Apocalypse use the source bypass. `AssetPack` invalidates clone poses per design/generation, and `SwfProtectionView` replaces the corresponding mask/splash while retaining live mounted artwork. Jam/stun gates can keep the old wrapper detached through travel; recharge retains its separate source behavior.
docs/architecture.md:174:The screen container uses the original world origin at stage275,225, outside camera zoom, field scale and transit offsets. `GameRenderer` starts it below the HUD, then raises it at crossing initiation, matching the original display-list reparenting. Collapse completion registers the original30-frame114 flash after starting the escape crossing. Mock mode paints simple stage rectangles using the imported root alpha curves; native mode paints the supplied vectors. Main-level shake and absolute constructor/stage phase alignment remain fidelity boundaries.
docs/architecture.md:312:`simulation/weapon-targets.ts` uses the same direct-reference target state as behaviors, separately for each offensive mount. Setup acquisition occurs during registered-part construction. The World parent tracker supplies attachment and cross-field projection; per-part Run/Aim acquisition and post-Aim ally-count refresh precede firing. `simulation/healer-target.ts` owns the distinct source Healer selector. Healing beams retain direct home and patient objects independently of part targets and active UID lookup. `Tank.dataActive` models the source NPC's cleared data while immutable design/render metadata and parentage survive. Part targets clear before data deactivation and the field's queued-removal callback. Known missing-ally and deactivated-data reads use a scoped World-listener error; retained art and later stage frames continue, while unrelated JavaScript errors escape. AddLife writes its signed integer life before reading data; a failed read leaves beam contact counter10 unchanged. Existing beams test parent presence rather than owner death/jam and remove themselves synchronously when either parent disappears. The tank-wide target selector and field-wide target resets are removed.
